\paragraph{Cross-environment scope.}
Our MATH and ALFWorld analyses (\S\ref{sec:discussion}) provide cross-environment evidence but do not conclusively establish generalization. In MATH (no death penalty), the deterministic advantage vanishes with a marginally significant reversal favoring internal reasoning ($+4.3$\,pp, $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). In ALFWorld, the deterministic advantage stems from an information gap (access to admissible actions) rather than death avoidance; moreover, 71.1\% of ALFWorld episodes result in both-strategy failure, limiting the counterfactual signal available from a 7B model on household tasks. Environments with graded failure costs or intermediate penalty regimes remain unstudied.

\paragraph{Reasoning strategy scope.}

\paragraph{Penalty sensitivity and generalization.}
The DAF's sensitivity to penalty magnitude---ranging from 50\% at $P{=}10$ to 98\% at $P{=}500$---highlights that death-avoidance dominance is not an invariant property of routing but a function of the environment's penalty structure. In domains with mild failure costs, non-death routing signals (e.g., reasoning quality differences on surviving episodes) would constitute a larger share of total routing value. This connects to reward shaping more broadly: the relative magnitude of catastrophic versus incremental penalties shapes which behavioral differences a router can exploit.

Our findings may also be capability-dependent. The 42.9\% internal-reasoning death rate reflects the failure mode of a 7B-parameter model prone to state hallucination. Larger models with lower hallucination rates would exhibit smaller death-rate differentials, potentially reducing DAF and revealing routing signal currently masked by catastrophic failures. Whether non-death routing signal \emph{emerges} as model capability increases---or remains negligible---is an open question with direct implications for the modality routing agenda.

\paragraph{Scope and future directions.}
Our primary analysis is grounded in a single interactive environment with a specific failure mode (the $-100$ death penalty), though the cross-environment evaluations on MATH ($N{=}300$) and ALFWorld ($N{=}135$) provide initial evidence that the counterfactual methodology generalizes: the directional routing trend on MATH and the information-asymmetry pattern on ALFWorld both demonstrate the framework's ability to identify environment-specific routing mechanisms. Extending to environments with graded failure costs, embodied agents with continuous action spaces, or multi-turn dialogue systems would further clarify how broadly death avoidance---or analogous catastrophic-failure avoidance---accounts for apparent routing signal. Step-level counterfactual labeling, rather than episode-level, is a complementary direction that could reveal finer-grained routing structure masked by our current aggregation. A further limitation is the gap between the implicit routing signal we measure---based on episode outcomes---and the representations an implicit router would use at inference time. Our router achieves above-chance step-level accuracy ($54.1\%$) but fails to generalize to held-out episodes ($47.1\%$), and data scaling ablations show this is not resolved by more data---the limitation is structural when always-deterministic is trivially near-optimal. Whether environments with more balanced routing signal (lower DAF) would yield learnable step-level routing remains an open question. Finally, validating these findings across model scales and families beyond 7--8B parameters would establish whether the death-avoidance dominance we observe is a property of the environment or of the capability regime.

