\begin{abstract}
Modular LLM agents that route between neural reasoning and deterministic computation promise improved reliability, yet understanding \emph{what} drives the value of routing decisions remains unclear. We propose episode-level counterfactual analysis: running complete episodes under pure strategies and comparing final outcomes to decompose routing signal into death-avoidance and non-death components. Among task types exhibiting meaningful routing signal in prescreening (11 of 30 available ScienceWorld task types, 245 episodes, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels (75 of 95) are driven by death asymmetry---a penalty-independent metric. Under the default penalty $P{=}100$, the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF) is $90.4\%$ (95\% CI: $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$), though DAF varies from 50\% ($P{=}10$) to 98\% ($P{=}500$) across penalty settings (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Per-task death rate correlates strongly with routing signal ($r{=}0.97$, though partly structural as death penalties contribute directly to score differences; \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}). An exploratory step-level router trained on these labels achieves $54.1 \pm 5.4\%$ step-level balanced accuracy (with same-episode leakage) but only $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$ on held-out episodes (below the always-deterministic baseline of 50\%); data scaling ($32{\times}$) and capacity scaling (0.6B--4B) confirm this is structural rather than data-limited. Cross-environment analysis on MATH ($N{=}300$) shows routing signal vanishes without a death penalty: the label distribution inverts from 86:9 (deterministic-preferred) to 30:17 (internal directionally favored), with a directional trend favoring internal reasoning ($+4.3$ percentage points, $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). A Llama-3.1-8B pilot provides preliminary directional evidence that death-driven routing generalizes across model families. These findings indicate that in environments with catastrophic failure modes, routing value is dominated by safety---the router functions primarily as a safety critic rather than an efficiency optimizer.
\end{abstract}

\paragraph{ALFWorld: information asymmetry without death.}
To further test the counterfactual framework's applicability, we evaluate on ALFWorld \citep{alfworld-2021}, a text-based household environment with 6 task types (json\_2.1.1 split, valid\_seen, 135 games, max 50 steps, binary success metric), using the same Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct backbone. Unlike ScienceWorld, ALFWorld has no death penalty or graded scoring---failure means exhausting the step budget. Crucially, the two modalities differ in \emph{information access} rather than computation mode: the internal strategy receives only free-text observations, while the deterministic strategy additionally receives a numbered list of admissible actions at each step, enabling direct action selection without free-form generation. This is an information asymmetry---access to an action-space oracle---rather than the simulator mode switch used in ScienceWorld. Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling} applies without modification; with binary outcomes, labeling reduces to success under one modality and failure under the other yielding a preference, otherwise no-preference.

The overall success rate gap is large: 27.4\% (deterministic) versus 3.0\% (internal), reflecting the 7B model's near-inability to solve household tasks without admissible-action guidance. The label distribution (Table~\ref{tab:cross-env}) shows 35 deterministic-preferred (25.9\%), 2 internal-preferred (1.5\%), and 98 no-preference (72.6\%), of which 96 are both-fail and only 2 both-succeed. Among episodes where at least one strategy succeeds, deterministic is preferred at a 35:2 ratio---but this advantage stems entirely from the information gap rather than death avoidance, confirming that the counterfactual framework correctly identifies different routing mechanisms across environments. Figure~\ref{fig:signal-reversal} and Table~\ref{tab:cross-env} summarize the cross-environment comparison.

